("v699", "type", "Sequence")
("v700", "type", "String")
("v700", "scalar", "This is where 1 Samuel 8 becomes unusually useful. The chapter begins with a concrete failure of judgment. Samuel appoints his sons as judges; they accept bribes and distort justice. The elders demand a king who will judge them and lead them in battle. Samuel warns that kingship will involve taking labor, land, produce, and household resources for royal purposes and royal beneficiaries. The people persist. The narrative also interprets their demand theologically as a rejection of divine kingship. These elements belong together in the passage; a purely administrative reading would leave out part of its account. (")
