("v939", "scalar", "Other passages complicate the picture usefully. Deuteronomy's account of kingship places the ruler under obligations and warns against accumulation and elevation above fellow Israelites. Read beside Samuel, it invites questions about the constraints attached to office rather than supplying a simple verdict that every centralized authority is intrinsically illegitimate. (")
("v938", "item", "v939")
